  "knowledge": {
    "progressSummary": "PROGRESS: proved sharp N/C bound [G:C_G(c)] ∣ p−1 for normal cyclic Sylow ⟨c⟩ (3 theorems, VERIFIED 0 sorry / 0 axiom); completes the centraliser-index nextStep.",
    "builtItems": [
      "zpowers_sylow_normal in AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean — arbitrary-prime Sylow-elimination core: order p·m + p∤m + no-small-divisor ⇒ ⟨c⟩ (order-p) normal",
      "conj_mem_zpowers_sylow — every g normalizes ⟨c⟩ (general prime consequence)",
      "zpowers_order5_normal now a one-line corollary of zpowers_sylow_normal (p=5 instance)",
      "zpowers_index_eq in AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean — [G:⟨c⟩]=m (quotient order m) by prime cancellation, no huniq/normality needed",
      "not_isSimpleGroup_of_sylow in AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean — arbitrary-prime non-simplicity from the normal Sylow core (1<m)",
      "not_isSimpleGroup_order5 + capstone example — p=5 corollary and 'any group of order 40 with an order-5 element is not simple'",
      "isSolvable_zpowers in AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean — a cyclic subgroup ⟨c⟩ is solvable (any two powers of c commute ⇒ abelian ⇒ isSolvable_of_comm)",
      "solvable_of_zpowers_normal in AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean — ⟨c⟩ normal + G⧸⟨c⟩ solvable ⇒ G solvable, via Mathlib's extension lemma solvable_of_ker_le_range with f=subtype, g=mk' (ker mk' = range subtype = ⟨c⟩)",
      "Order-10 solvability capstone example — order 10 = 5·2 with an order-5 element is solvable: zpowers_order5_normal gives normality, zpowers_index_eq gives quotient order 2, isCyclic_of_prime_card + IsCyclic.commutative make the quotient solvable",
      "conj_pow_eq_zpow_pow (AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean): pure-group-theory iteration g^j c g^{-j}=c^(k^j) from g c g⁻¹=c^k, no finiteness/Sylow hyps",
      "conj_exponent_pow_modEq_one (AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean): under zpowers_sylow_normal hyps, g^n=1 ⇒ ∃k, g c g⁻¹=c^k ∧ k^n≡1 [ZMOD p] — automorphism-order-agnostic generalisation of involution_conj_eq_self_or_inv (VERIFIED 0/0, PR pending)",
      "conjNormal_ker_eq_centralizer (AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean:276): kernel of the conjugation action G →* Aut(⟨c⟩) equals the centralizer C_G(c); no finiteness needed, only normality of ⟨c⟩",
      "conjNormal_ker_index_dvd_sub_one (AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean:308): index of that kernel divides p−1 via IsCyclic.card_mulAut (|Aut(⟨c⟩)|=φ(p)=p−1), first-iso + Lagrange; Normal taken as instance binder so the statement is well-formed",
      "centralizer_index_dvd_sub_one (AbelRuffiniOQ04OQ01OQ03.lean:334): the sharp N/C bound [G:C_G(c)] ∣ p−1 for a normal cyclic Sylow ⟨c⟩ of prime order p"
    ],
    "insights": [
      "Generalizing the fixed prime 5 to a variable prime p is NOT a rename: the two number-theoretic steps change tactic. n_p ≡ 1 (mod p) ⇒ n_p = 1 needs Nat.mod_eq_of_lt hp.out.one_lt to evaluate 1 % p (omega cannot do variable-modulus mod); index cancellation [G:P]·p = p·m ⇒ [G:P] = m needs Nat.eq_of_mul_eq_mul_right (omega cannot cancel a variable multiplier).",
      "The whole Sylow API (Sylow.card_eq_multiplicity, card_sylow_modEq_one, Sylow.card_dvd_index, IsPGroup.exists_le_sylow) is already prime-generic, so the group-theory skeleton transports verbatim; only the two ℕ-arithmetic steps required new lemmas.",
      "The normal Sylow p-subgroup is not just an intermediate step: it is a proper nontrivial normal subgroup, so any group of order p·m (p∤m, no small Frobenius divisor, 1<m) with an order-p element is NOT simple. This is the structural reason the x⁵-4x+2 Galois groups (orders 10,20,40) cannot be the simple A₅.",
      "IsSimpleGroup.eq_bot_or_eq_top_of_normal turns non-simplicity into a clean two-case refutation: ⟨c⟩=⊥ contradicts orderOf c=p>1 (via Subgroup.zpowers_eq_bot + orderOf_one), ⟨c⟩=⊤ contradicts index=m>1 (via Subgroup.index_top).",
      "The index [G:⟨c⟩]=m follows by pure counting (index_mul_card + Nat.eq_of_mul_eq_mul_right cancelling the prime p) with NO divisor hypothesis and NO normality — it just records that G⧸⟨c⟩ has order m.",
      "The quotient type G⧸(Subgroup.zpowers c) only has a Group instance once (zpowers c).Normal is in scope, so any theorem whose hypothesis mentions IsSolvable (G⧸⟨c⟩) must carry the Normal instance in its binder list (or establish it via haveI before the hypothesis is used). The clean reusable form takes [(Subgroup.zpowers c).Normal] as an instance argument rather than re-deriving it from Sylow hypotheses.",
      "Solvability is the positive dual of non-simplicity here: ⟨c⟩ cyclic (abelian, solvable) + solvable quotient ⇒ G solvable by the standard extension lemma. For the Abel–Ruffini orders 10,20,40 the quotients have order 2,4,8 (all 2-groups, hence solvable), so those Galois groups are solvable — the structural reason x⁵-4x+2's group must instead be the non-solvable S₅ for radical-insolubility.",
      "Conjugation by any g on the normal cyclic ⟨c⟩ (order p) is an element of Aut(⟨c⟩)≅(ℤ/p)ˣ whose order divides ord(g): the exponent k with g c g⁻¹=c^k satisfies k^n≡1 (mod p) whenever g^n=1. The involution ±1 lemma is exactly the n=2 case, where primality upgrades k²≡1 to k≡±1.",
      "The sharp N/C refinement of the ±1 involution lemma: conjugation on a normal cyclic ⟨c⟩ of prime order p factors through Aut(⟨c⟩)≅(ℤ/p)ˣ of order p−1, so C_G(c) has index dividing p−1 (Noether first-iso + Lagrange). The involution lemma is the order-2 special case; this is the full automorphism-group constraint."
    ],
